WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.060
The client lifecycle, the client record, and your starting point.

2
00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:08.700
This is the video for module 1 of Client Relationship Management.

3
00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:21.280
Client relationship management, or CRM, is the set of habits and records you use to look after people who already buy from you, so they stay, buy again and recommend you.

4
00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:32.280
A tailor with a good notebook and a steady follow-up routine is doing CRM. A business with expensive software that nobody updates is not.

5
00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:37.680
This module shows you the full cycle, what to record, and where you are starting from.

6
00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:44.100
First, enquiry. She asks a question, sees your Status, or a friend refers her.

7
00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:52.660
Second, onboarding. You agree the item, the price, the deposit and the date. This is where expectations are set.

8
00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:57.800
Third, delivery. You do the work and record what was delivered.

9
00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:08.900
Fourth, after-care. A short check that she is happy, a care tip, a thank-you. Most small businesses skip this stage, and that is where they lose repeat clients.

10
00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:17.060
Fifth, repeat and referral. She orders again or sends a friend, and the cycle starts again with more trust.

11
00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:24.420
Client lifetime value is how much one client is likely to spend with you over the whole time she stays.

12
00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:29.760
The formula is average order, times orders per year, times the years she stays.

13
00:01:31.010 --> 00:01:34.830
Bisi's average party order is about 25,000 naira.

14
00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:41.620
Her regular clients order about 3 times a year. That is 75,000 naira a year.

15
00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:48.680
They stay about 4 years. So one loyal client is worth about 300,000 naira.

16
00:01:49.930 --> 00:01:55.770
A one-time client is worth 25,000. A loyal one is worth twelve times as much.

17
00:01:56.120 --> 00:02:05.570
So Bisi decides a thank-you message and a reminder before the festive season is well worth a few minutes. Use your own real numbers, not a guess.

18
00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:12.880
Once you have more than about ten clients, memory starts to fail. So give every client one record.

19
00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:21.100
Write her name, phone and how she found you. Her order history with amounts. Her preferences, and the important dates she mentions.

20
00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:33.850
Record every promise you made, like a free adjustment. And end with one next action and a date. A record without a next action is just history. With one, it becomes a plan.

21
00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:42.270
Words like difficult are opinions. They do not tell your future self what to do, and they could offend a client who saw them.

22
00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:49.410
A useful note is specific, factual, dated and short. It records what she said or did.

23
00:02:49.760 --> 00:03:03.430
For example, Amaka, a tailor in Enugu, notes that Chioma was referred by Nkechi, dislikes sleeveless styles, and starts a new job on 1 July. Next action: fitting reminder on 20 June.

24
00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:07.600
Update the record within 24 hours, while details are fresh.

25
00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:14.030
First, take the ten-point self-audit in the lesson, and write your score down with the date.

26
00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:18.440
Then choose a tool. A paper notebook works for a handful of clients.

27
00:03:19.690 --> 00:03:30.910
Google Sheets is the best free step up. For example, Funmi sells liquid soap in Abeokuta to 46 clients. She scored 3 out of 10, so she chose Google Sheets.

28
00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:43.190
WhatsApp Business labels organise your daily chats, but they are not a full record. For most of us, the best mix is a sheet plus labels, with one single home for records.

29
00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:48.900
Your client list is valuable and private. Set a screen lock on your phone.

30
00:03:50.150 --> 00:03:55.950
Turn on two-step verification in WhatsApp, so no one can register your number on another phone.

31
00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:02.110
Use a strong password on your Google account and turn on 2-Step Verification.

32
00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:10.310
And do not record things unrelated to serving her, like gossip or health details she did not share for the order.

33
00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:17.110
Never share a verification code, even with someone claiming to be from WhatsApp or your bank.

34
00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:32.480
To remember. CRM is habits first. Watch the after-care stage. Know what a loyal client is worth. Keep one factual record per client, ending in a next action and date. And secure your phone and accounts.

35
00:04:33.730 --> 00:04:47.510
Now practise. Calculate your own client lifetime value and circle your weakest stage. Write full records for your three most recent clients. Then take the audit, choose your tool and turn on your security settings.

36
00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:49.800
Continue with the lessons below.
